# Airline Commercial Strategist Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Lens:** The airport's fate is more determined by carrier decisions than by anything the airport itself does. A quiet-by-design standard for IAD and DCA is not a design document — it is a term in the 2025–2039 airline use agreement, whether MWAA writes it that way or not. This brief tests the thesis against one question: **what will United and American do in response?**

---

## 1. Key findings

1. **The airlines own most of the noise.** Under standard hold-room practice, boarding calls, upgrade lists, standby paging, and delay announcements originate with the airline gate agent on airline-controlled equipment. MWAA can specify the acoustic envelope, PA hardware, and zone geometry; it cannot unilaterally rewrite the announcement scripts an airline employee reads out. A standard that mistakes airport turf for airline turf will die in the first drafting session with United and American.
2. **The 15-year Use Agreement (effective January 1, 2025) is the operative instrument.** Any quiet-by-design requirement that materially changes airline premises, tenant paging rights, or the capital program engages majority-in-interest (MII) consultation and consent. MWAA does not get to add a $50M ambient-acoustics scope to Concourse E without airline sign-off in the mechanism the 2024 agreement defines.
3. **United designed the Dulles program and holds the pen at IAD.** CEO Scott Kirby has publicly stated United "designed it by and large." United ran ~250 daily departures at IAD in 2024, planning ~280 by April 2025, with 9.2M boardings in 2025 (+7% YoY). Concourse E's 14 gates and the 40,000 sq ft United Club are United's real estate. Any acoustic standard applied to Concourse E is negotiated with United, not imposed on it.
4. **American holds a ~53% share at DCA — a fortress-hub-grade position.** That is more than 5x its nearest competitor by route count. Under a first-principles MII reading, American alone is close to a controlling vote on anything that touches DCA capital budgets or the terminals it dominates. A DCA quiet standard without American's consent is not a standard; it is a wish.
5. **The CPE arithmetic is the veto.** MWAA's own 2025 Official Statement projected IAD CPE rising to $20.71 by 2030 before the $20B+ July 2026 announcement. A May 2026 airline briefing on the revised program projected IAD CPE reaching **$90.64 by 2035** (Kirby has publicly disputed this on volume-dilution grounds). Against that headline number, any acoustics or PA-zoning scope that adds even $10–30M of program cost will be scrutinized against the marginal CPE it drives. Airlines do not fight $2M standards line-by-line; they fight the aggregate that puts them into the fifth quintile nationally.
6. **The two airports have different carrier operating models and cannot share the same PA regime.** IAD is a global connecting hub; the median passenger spends time in a hold room during a bank between arrivals and departures, benefits from digital wayfinding, and — for international connections — has heightened language and accessibility needs. DCA is an O&D transcon + regional operation with short dwells, walk-up passengers, and now (post-2024 FAA Reauthorization) five new beyond-perimeter transcon peaks that stretch its 1997 terminal footprint. One announcement cadence will not fit both.
7. **International "quiet airport" peers are not carrier-neutral analogs.** Helsinki-Vantaa (Finavia's "silent airport," 2019) works because Finnair is essentially the only airline in the concourse and Finavia coordinated the policy with it directly. Schiphol works because KLM is the coordinating carrier. At IAD, United is the coordinating carrier; at DCA, American is. Any peer benchmark that skips this fact is branding, not evidence.
8. **The historical warning: airports that hard-specified terminals to airline preferences that then changed got left holding the bonds.** CVG, PIT, STL, MEM, and CLE all built to a carrier operating model that the carrier walked away from within a decade of debt issuance. A quiet-by-design standard hard-coded to United's 2026 hub structure is a bet that United still runs IAD the same way in 2039. That is not a safe bet, and the standard should be written to survive carrier reconfiguration.

---

## 2. Evidence

### 2.1 The airline structure at MWAA is asymmetric — and asymmetry is the whole story

At IAD, United is the dominant hub carrier operating from Concourses A/B (regional), C, and D, and — from Fall 2026 — the new 14-gate, 435,000 sq ft Concourse E, with a 40,000 sq ft United Club [Source: https://www.businesstraveller.com/business-travel/2024/12/04/washington-dulles-concourse-e-to-open-in-late-2026/]. United's own communications describe the Concourse E program as "more than a half a billion-dollar expansion & modernization" of its Dulles hub [Source: https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/more-than-half-a-billion-dollar-expansion--modernization-coming-to-uniteds-washington-dulles-hub-302320375.html]. Scott Kirby's public position on the July 2026 $22.5B announcement — "We designed it by and large" — is not marketing [Source: https://byerussell.substack.com/p/uniteds-vision-for-dulles-is-a-go]. That statement establishes the negotiating baseline: any MWAA design manual provision at IAD is a provision United has already, informally, blessed or blocked.

At DCA, American Airlines holds a **~53% market share** and more than five times the route count of its nearest competitor [Source: https://simpleflying.com/american-airlines-washington-dc-routes-analysis-march-2025/]. Slot-controlled airports concentrate power; DCA's slot-and-perimeter regime concentrates it further. The five 2024-authorized beyond-perimeter slot pairs (Alaska/SAN, American/SAT, Delta/SEA, Southwest/LAS, United/SFO) added transcon peaks and larger gauge to a terminal already at its physical envelope [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504], but did not meaningfully dilute American's control of the building.

### 2.2 The use agreement mechanics

The 15-year Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease approved in 2024 requires signatory carriers representing a majority of flights to sign before taking effect, and bundles the operative capital program into the same instrument [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. The public reporting establishes an original $6.99B capital program at IAD and $2.39B at DCA. The July 2026 announcement replaces the IAD number with something on the order of $22.5B [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/07/31/dulles-partners-mostly-mum-on-details-of-how-22-5b-expansion-plan-will-be-funded/].

Under industry-standard MII architecture, a typical provision requires approval by 50%+1 of signatory airlines *and* 50%+1 of activity (landed weight or enplanements), with supermajority thresholds (60–67%) for large capital programs or bond issuances [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airline-use-agreements]. Miami International's use agreement is illustrative of the CPE-tripwire structure: if CPE exceeds $35 (in 2018 dollars) in 6+ years of the 10-year projection period, MWAA-equivalent capital projects must seek MII approval [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airline-use-agreements]. MWAA's specific thresholds are in the 2024 agreement, which was not fully retrievable in this stage, but the structural point holds: **at the CPE trajectory that MWAA itself has published, MII is not a hypothetical constraint on capital additions to the design standard — it is the binding one.**

### 2.3 The Dulles CPE math is the real gate on the standard

MWAA's July 2025 Official Statement forecasted IAD CPE rising to **$20.71 by 2030** before the July 2026 announcement [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/dulles-rfi-analysis]. Post-announcement, a May 2026 airline briefing on the full program showed IAD CPE reaching **$90.64 by 2035** under the $22.5B envelope [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. Kirby has publicly disputed the $90 number, arguing volume growth ("more Es") dilutes per-unit cost [Source: https://byerussell.substack.com/p/uniteds-vision-for-dulles-is-a-go]. Both numbers matter for the standard: the $20.71 baseline is what governs the CPE test today; the $90.64 tail is what governs airline appetite for *any* scope addition that is not clearly essential.

For reference, large-hub CPE nationally ranges $8–18 with outliers into the $20s at hubs undergoing major capital programs [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/cost-per-enplaned-passenger]. At $20.71, IAD is already at the top-quintile edge. At $90.64, IAD would be a global outlier, and carriers would be actively modeling substitution — either into DCA (impossible under slots) or into competing East Coast connecting hubs (EWR, JFK, BOS, ATL, CLT, PHL). A quiet-by-design standard that adds even 2–3% to program cost is meaningful in that regime. A standard priced honestly at $10–40M — realistic for STI/RT60 targets, DSP-driven directional PA, hold-room zone speakers, and commissioning — is not free money in this CPE environment.

### 2.4 What the airlines actually control at the gate

ACRP Research Report 175 — the industry primary reference on airport PA intelligibility — establishes the design metric (Speech Transmission Index, STI ≥ 0.50 typical for intelligibility) and documents that airport terminals frequently fail to reach it because of reverberant finishes, background noise, and poorly zoned loudspeaker geometry [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. That research is airport-facing on the *envelope* (finishes, RT60, ambient noise, loudspeaker layout). It does not — and cannot — dictate what the airline gate agent says into the microphone.

The operational division is real: gate agents are airline employees whose job explicitly includes making departure announcements and boarding calls [Source: https://navan.com/resources/glossary/what-is-gate-agent]. Practice at working airports confirms that the timing of call-to-gate announcements is determined by an agreement between the airline and the airport [Source: https://airtraveldesign.guide/PA-Announcements]. That is the seam MWAA has to negotiate across, not design around.

### 2.5 The missed-boarding economics create real airline downside

Airlines closely track D0 (on-time push at zero minutes past scheduled), and gate agents are increasingly motivated to close doors promptly rather than hold for stragglers [Source: https://creators.yahoo.com/lifestyle/story/they-boarded-and-left-15-minutes-early-delta-passengers-missed-flight-is-a-lesson-for-all-travelers-190958318.html]. That operational pressure creates the risk that a *narrower* announcement policy transfers the cost of "did not hear the call" to the airline in the form of missed boardings, downline rebooking, checked-bag pull-and-reload cycles, and Denied Boarding compensation exposure. SFO's quiet policy has drawn passenger complaint and adjustment [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. That downside cost is not the airport's — it is United's and American's — and it is exactly the kind of cost that shows up in a signatory carrier's technical objection during the design review.

### 2.6 International peer "quiet airport" evidence must be corrected for carrier concentration

Helsinki-Vantaa's silent-airport policy (Finavia, from June 2019) eliminated terminal-wide flight and passenger announcements while preserving gate-area and emergency announcements [Source: https://morepremium.com/helsinki-vantaa-to-be-made-a-quiet-airport/]. It works in part because Finnair is functionally the single flag carrier in the concourse and Finavia coordinated the policy with it directly. Schiphol is similar with KLM. IAD is superficially analogous — United dominates — but is unlike HEL in that IAD has ~50 daily flights operated by 20+ foreign flag carriers on Concourse A/B international arrivals, each with their own communications culture and language mix. DCA is unlike HEL in a different way: no single carrier operates a "quiet airport" concourse well when its own gate agents are pushing D0 on 40+ transcon and regional turns per day.

### 2.7 The dehubbing case as risk framing

CVG, PIT, STL, MEM, and CLE each built terminal capacity to a carrier operating model that the carrier subsequently walked away from — Delta thinned CVG connecting banks after the Northwest merger; US Airways ran down PIT after 2004; TWA/American compressed STL; Northwest/Delta compressed MEM (formally dehubbed 2013); Continental/United thinned CLE (dehubbed 2014). In each case, the airport's capital plan assumed a persistent airline commitment that the airline's route economics eventually rejected. The point for a quiet-by-design standard is not that United will dehub IAD — the July 2026 announcement is a strong signal against that on a 10-year horizon — but that a standard should be **portable across carrier operating models**, not tuned to United's 2026 hub structure. If the standard makes hold-room acoustic performance dependent on a specific PA architecture that only fits United's current banking pattern, MWAA is doing the same thing CVG did with its E concourse: buying capacity to a set of assumptions the carrier does not owe you.

