## 3. Case studies of carrier responses to airport decisions

**Case 1 — CVG, Delta's post-2008 unwinding.** Delta inherited CVG through the Northwest merger and did not want a second Midwest connecting hub alongside DTW and MSP. Kenton County had built the Concourse A/B/C system on a hub premise. Delta thinned the banks, cut fifth-freedom-style connecting flow, and CVG's CPE spiked as fixed costs got amortized over a shrinking enplanement base. The airport recovered by winning cargo (Amazon Air) — a different business entirely. *Lesson for MWAA:* a physical standard tuned to a specific carrier's operating model is a bet on that model.

**Case 2 — PIT, US Airways' 2004 dehubbing.** US Airways had committed to PIT in the mid-1990s when Allegheny County built the airside/landside terminal. Post-bankruptcy, US Airways walked. PIT is still adapting the airfield and terminal 20 years later. *Lesson for MWAA:* the airport-side execution of a carrier's stated preference is not enforceable on the carrier.

**Case 3 — SFO's quiet policy.** SFO adopted a quiet policy and estimated a ~40% decrease in "unnecessary noise" and up to 90 minutes/day of announcement time removed. The policy has generated visible passenger complaint and airline gate-level adaptation [Source: https://simpleflying.com/airport-quiet-policy-frustrating-passengers-sfo/]. This is the closest US analog to what MWAA is contemplating and the useful reference point — not Helsinki.

**Case 4 — DEN's 2018 quiet policy.** DEN implemented a quiet airport policy and has since had to actively manage its performance during holiday peaks, where the missed-boarding risk profile changes [Source: https://www.denver7.com/lifestyle/travel/denver-international-airports-quiet-policy-being-put-to-the-test-during-busy-holiday-travel-season]. The evidence to look for at DEN is whether Frontier, Southwest, and United's D0 or missed-boarding metrics moved after adoption. That data was not retrieved at Stage 1 and is an evidence gap.

